// theorem-demo — a comprehensive tour of the `theorem` package.
//
// The package layers amsthm-style theorem environments over the counters package.
// Every public feature is exercised below:
//
//   * \newtheorem{env}{Heading}            — an environment with its own counter
//   * \newtheorem{env}[shared]{Heading}    — share another environment's counter
//   * \newtheorem{env}{Heading}[within]    — number within a counter (e.g. section)
//   * \begin{env}[note] ... \end{env}      — an optional parenthesised note in the head
//   * the proof environment with its run-in "Proof." head
//   * \qed                                 — the flush-right end-of-proof box
//
// We pull in `document` for section headings and paragraph indentation, and `math`
// for the formulas set inside the theorem bodies.

\use{document}
\use{theorem}
\use{math}

// Each \newtheorem defines a fresh environment. `theorem` gets its own counter.
// `lemma` and `corollary` SHARE the theorem counter (the [theorem] optional before
// the heading), so all three advance one running sequence. `definition` and
// `example` are numbered WITHIN each section (the [section] optional after the
// heading), printing "section.n". `remark` keeps an independent counter of its own.
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\newtheorem{corollary}[theorem]{Corollary}
\newtheorem{definition}{Definition}[section]
\newtheorem{example}{Example}[section]
\newtheorem{remark}{Remark}

\title{The \texttt{theorem} Package}
\author{A tour of amsthm-style environments in texish}
\maketitle

\section{Independent numbering}

A theorem-like environment defined with \texttt{\\newtheorem\{theorem\}\{Theorem\}}
carries its own counter. The plain style sets a bold run-in head and an italic body,
and the body shape reverts at \texttt{\\end}.

\begin{theorem}
The square of the hypotenuse of a right triangle equals the sum of the squares of
its legs: $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$. This is Theorem 1, the first member of its own counter.
\end{theorem}

A second instance advances the same counter to become Theorem 2.

\begin{theorem}
Every integer $n > 1$ has a factorisation into primes, $n = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_k$,
which is unique up to the order of the factors.
\end{theorem}

\section{Shared counters}

The optional argument \emph{before} the heading shares another environment's counter.
Here \texttt{lemma} and \texttt{corollary} were both declared with \texttt{[theorem]},
so they continue the single running sequence rather than starting fresh. Note the
numbers below pick up where the theorems left off.

\begin{lemma}
A lemma sharing the theorem counter numbers in the same sequence, so this is Lemma 3
rather than Lemma 1. If $f$ is differentiable on $(a, b)$ and continuous on $[a, b]$,
then $f$ attains a maximum on $[a, b]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}
A corollary, also sharing that counter, follows as Corollary 4. Hence between any two
distinct reals there lies a rational number.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}
And a further theorem advances the shared sequence once more, to Theorem 5.
\end{theorem}

\section{Optional notes}

A note in brackets after \texttt{\\begin} is shown in parentheses in the head, giving
the theorem a name. The body should start on the line after \texttt{\\begin} so that a
normal interword space follows the run-in head.

\begin{theorem}[Pythagoras]
For a right triangle with legs $a$, $b$ and hypotenuse $c$, the squares of the legs
sum to the square of the hypotenuse: $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Schwarz inequality]
For vectors $u$ and $v$ in an inner-product space,
$\langle u, v \rangle^2 \le \langle u, u \rangle \, \langle v, v \rangle$, with
equality exactly when $u$ and $v$ are linearly dependent.
\end{lemma}

\section{Numbering within a section}

Environments declared with a trailing \texttt{[section]} reset whenever the section
counter advances and print the section number as a prefix. So the items below are
numbered 4.1, 4.2, and so on, the leading 4 being the number of this section.

\begin{definition}[Continuity]
A function $f$ is continuous at a point $x_0$ when small changes in $x$ near $x_0$
produce arbitrarily small changes in $f(x)$. This is Definition 4.1.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Limit]
The limit $\lim_{x \to x_0} f(x) = L$ means that $f(x)$ can be made as close to $L$
as desired by taking $x$ sufficiently close to $x_0$. This numbers as Definition 4.2.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
The function $f(x) = x^2$ is continuous everywhere. Because \texttt{example} also
numbers within the section but on its OWN counter, it begins at 4.1 independently of
the definitions above.
\end{example}

\section{Proofs and the QED mark}

The \texttt{proof} environment sets an italic run-in ``Proof.'' head with an upright
body. Placing \texttt{\\qed} at the end sets the end-of-proof box flush right on
the last line. Definitions in this section continue the within-section numbering, now
prefixed with 5.

\begin{definition}[Even integer]
An integer $n$ is even when $n = 2k$ for some integer $k$. This is Definition 5.1,
the section prefix having advanced.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
The sum of two even integers is even.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $m = 2j$ and $n = 2k$ for integers $j$ and $k$. Then $m + n = 2j + 2k = 2(j + k)$,
which is twice the integer $j + k$, and is therefore even. \qed
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Irrationality of \texttt{root 2}]
There is no rational number whose square is $2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $\sqrt{2} = p / q$ in lowest terms. Then $p^2 = 2 q^2$, so $p$ is even, say
$p = 2r$; then $2 q^2 = 4 r^2$, so $q$ is even too. But then $p$ and $q$ share the
factor $2$, contradicting lowest terms. \qed
\end{proof}

\section{Independent counters revisited}

Finally, the \texttt{remark} environment was given neither a shared counter nor a
within-section parent, so it runs entirely on its own, unaffected by the theorems,
definitions, and examples above.

\begin{remark}
Remarks number independently of every other environment, so this is Remark 1.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
And the next remark is simply Remark 2.
\end{remark}
